  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1258 AM CDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nSmoke is now the main forecast concern with the RRFS bringing the \nplume of near surface smoke down out of Montana/North Dakota into \ncentral/north central and northeast South Dakota during the early \nmorning into the daytime hours. There is a weak surface high \ncirculation to the north, so as the high continues east, winds \nshift east and then southeast. Thus the smoke moves in and then \nlingers across the area even into Tuesday though over time it will \nbe tempered by deep mixing. Upper level smoke is likewise expected \nto spill over the ridge into the area. This will have a feedback on \ntemperatures, but with the slightly 'cooler' airmass coming in \nunsure what this will do in comparison with the NBM. \n\nAnother plume of moisture pivoting around the upper high from the \nIntermountain West crosses into South Dakota tonight. CAMS show \ngenerally weak convection, with skinny CAPE up above 10kts and an \ninverted V type profile. \n\nThe ridge continues to retrograde slowly with the upper high slowly \nflattening. With the ridge weakening, 700mb temperatures drop from \ntheir peak down to around a standard deviation above climo, though \nthis anomalously warm air aloft sticks with us through the work week. \nLate in the week and for the weekend the ridge breaks down in the \npresence of a wave crossing the northern tier CONUS.",
